          %title Quandles
          %h1#quandles Quandles
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_idea"} The Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#links"} Links
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#the_idea The Idea
          %p
            A quandle is a set equipped with a binary operation satisfying axioms analogous to the three
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reidemeister+moves"} Reidemeister moves
            in knot theory. A quandle is a special case of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rack"} rack
          %p
            While mainly used to obtain invariants of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knots
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong quandle
            is a selfdistributive idempotent right
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasigroup"} quasigroup
            %strong quandle
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rack"} rack
            obeying the law
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright a = a
          %p or equivalently
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleleft a = a \, .
          %p
            In other words, a quandle is a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            equipped with two binary operations,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▹
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleleft
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ▹
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright (b \triangleright c) = (a \triangleright b)\triangleright (a \triangleright  c)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ◃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c \triangleleft b) \triangleleft a = (c \triangleleft a)\triangleleft (b \triangleleft a)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \triangleright b)\triangleleft a = b
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright (b \triangleleft a) = b
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright a = a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleleft a = a
          %p
            Given laws 3 and 4, the operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▹
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright
            determines the operation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleleft
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rack"} rack
            for more discussion of related points.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            gives a quandle where the operations come from conjugation:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright b = a b a^{-1}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi b
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \triangleleft a = a^{-1} b a
          %p
            In fact, every equational law satisfied by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
            in a group follows from the quandle axioms. So, one can think of a quandle as what is left of a group when we forget multiplication, the identity, and inverses, and only remember the operation of conjugation.
          %p
            Every tame knot in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            has a “fundamental quandle”. To define this, one can note that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            of the knot complement, or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+group"} knot group
            = succeed ")" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Wirtinger presentation
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Wirtinger+presentation"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            fundamental quandles then there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
          %p
            Less powerful but more easily computable invariants of knots may be obtained by counting the homomorphisms from the knot quandle to a fixed quandle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+diagram"} knot diagram
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
          %p
            The Alexander quandles are also important, since they can be used to compute the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+polynomial"} Alexander polynomial
            of a knot. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            be a module over the ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[t, t^{-1}]
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+polynomial"} Laurent polynomials
            in one variable. Then the
            %strong Alexander quandle
            consists of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            made into a quandle with the left action given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright b = t a + (1-t)b
          %p
            Analogous to how evaluating the Alexander polynomial at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = -1
            (and then taking absolute value) defines the
            %em determinant
            of a knot, similarly, instantiating the Alexander quandle at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = -1
            gives rise to the
            %strong dihedral quandle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi b
                  %mo −
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright b = 2b - a
          %p
            which, when interpreted as an action on the ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_n
            of integers modulo
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colorable+knot"} n-colorability
            of a knot.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rack"} Racks
            are a useful generalization of quandles in topology, since while quandles can represent knots on a round linear object (such as rope or a thread), racks can represent ribbons, which may be twisted as well as knotted.
          %p
            A quandle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is said to be
            %strong involutory
            if it obeys the law
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright (a \triangleright b) = b
          %p or equivalently
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b \triangleleft a) \triangleleft a = b
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+space"} symmetric space
            gives an involutory quandle, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright b
            is the result of ‘reflecting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            through
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rack"} racks
            are already algebras of a Lawvere theory, and involutory quandles are racks obeying some extra equational laws. We may thus define involutory quandle objects in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            in the category of smooth manifolds with the additional properties that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                each point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is an
                %em isolated
                fixed point of the operation
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ▹
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \triangleright -
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                is connected.
          %p This fact is Theorem I.4.3. in:
          %ul
            %li
              Wolgang Bertram,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Geometry of Jordan and Lie Structures
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 1754
          %p He attributes this result to:
          %ul
            %li
              Ottmar Loos,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symmetric Spaces I
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rack"} rack
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shelf"} shelf
          %h2#links Links
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia:
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racks_and_quandles"} Racks and quandles
            %li
              %p
                Gavin Wraith,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.wra1th.plus.com/gcw/math/Rack.html"} A personal story about knots
          %h2#references References
          %p Surveys are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                S. Nelson,
                %em What is a Quandle?
                , Notices AMS
                %strong 63
                no.4 (2016) pp.378-380. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/publications/journals/notices/201604/rnoti-p378.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                J. Scott Carter,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A survey of quandle ideas
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1002.4429"} arxiv
          %p A monograph is
          %ul
            %li
              M. Elhamdadi, S. Nelson,
              %em Quandles: An Introduction to the Algebra of Knots
              , AMS Providence 2015. (
              %a{:href => "http://bookstore.ams.org/stml-74"}> link
              )
          %p
            The paper by Crans makes it clear that quandles are algebras of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            (a category with finite
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebras
          %ul
            %li
              Alissa Crans,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Shelves, racks, spindles and quandles
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/math/pdf/0409/0409602v1.pdf#page=56"} arXiv
              = succeed ")." do
                %em Lie 2-Algebras
          %p Other research papers:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                David Joyce,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A classifying invariant of knots; the knot quandle
                %strong 23
                (1982), 37-65,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(82)90077-9"} doi
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=638121"} MR83m:57007
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022404982900779/pdf?md5=cc81b2cf5a01afc9277d58d10128878a&pid=1-s2.0-0022404982900779-main.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                David Joyce,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Simple quandles
                %strong 79
                (1982), no. 2, 307–318,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0021-8693(82)90305-2"} doi
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=682881"} MR84d:20078
            %li
              %p
                Seiichi Kamada,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Knot invariants derived from quandles and racks
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0211096"} arXiv
            %li
              %p
                J. Scott Carter, Masahico Saito,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quandle homology theory and cocycle knot invariants
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0112026"} arXiv
            %li
              %p
                Michael Eisermann,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quandle coverings and their Galois correspondence
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.igt.uni-stuttgart.de/eiserm/publications/qcovering.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Aaron Kaestner, Sam Nelson, Leo Selker,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Parity biquandle invariants of virtual knots
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1507.05583"} arxiv/1507.05583
            %li
              %p
                Dominique Bourn,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Partial Mal’tsevness and category of quandles
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://ct2015.web.ua.pt/abstracts/bourn_d.pdf"} abstract
                %a{:href => "http://ct2015.web.ua.pt/slides/Bourn.pdf"} slides pdf
